[1/92] GPQA Diamond/Physics An intelligent civilization in the Large Magellanic Cloud has
We need to compute the proper time experienced by the astronaut traveling at v = 0.99999987c. The distance from the Large Magellanic Cloud to Earth is about 160,000 light-years. However, the problem doesn't give the distance explicitly—it gives the aliens' average lifetime (150 solar years) and the astronaut's age (22 years). The question is about whether the astronaut will survive the trip.

The key is relativistic time dilation. For the astronaut, the time experienced (proper time) is the Earth-frame travel time divided by the Lorentz factor γ.

γ = 1 / sqrt(1 - v²/c²)  
v/c = 0.99999987  
1 - (v/c)² = 1 - (0.99999987)²  
= 1 - 0.9999997400001369 ≈ 2.59999863 × 10⁻⁷  
So γ ≈ 1 / sqrt(2.6e-7) ≈ 1 / (5.099e-4) ≈ 1961.

If the distance to LMC is about 160,000 light-years, then Earth-frame travel time = distance / v ≈ 160,000 / 0.99999987 ≈ 160,000 years (slightly more).  
Proper time for astronaut = 160,000 / 1961 ≈ 81.6 years.

The astronaut is 22 years old, so after 81.6 years proper time, he will be about 103.6 years old. The average alien lifetime is 150 years, so he will survive (barely). But the question asks: "Approximately, how long will it take for their 22 years old astronaut (from the point of view of the astronaut) to reach the Earth?" That is the proper time, ~81.6 years, so closest is 81 years.

Thus answer is B.

Answer: B
