[10/92] GPQA Diamond/Physics For a magnetic field \vec{B}, parallel to Oz, with small values
We are asked to compare the paramagnetic coupling term \(\langle H \rangle\) with the transition energy \(\Delta E\) for a hydrogen atom transition with wavelength \(\lambda = 0.4861 \, \mu\text{m}\).

First, compute \(\Delta E\) from the wavelength:
\[
\Delta E = \frac{hc}{\lambda}
\]
Using \(h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J·s}\), \(c = 3.0 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\), \(\lambda = 0.4861 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m}\):
\[
\Delta E = \frac{(6.626 \times 10^{-34})(3.0 \times 10^8)}{0.4861 \times 10^{-6}} \approx \frac{1.9878 \times 10^{-25}}{4.861 \times 10^{-7}} \approx 4.09 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J}
\]
In eV (1 eV = \(1.602 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J}\)):  
\(\Delta E \approx 2.55 \, \text{eV}\).

Now the paramagnetic coupling term for a magnetic field \(B = 1 \, \text{T}\) along Oz, with small \(m\) (orbital magnetic quantum number). The Zeeman term is:
\[
H_{\text{paramagnetic}} = -\vec{\mu} \cdot \vec{B} = -\mu_B \, m_l \, B
\]
where \(\mu_B = \frac{e\hbar}{2m_e} \approx 9.274 \times 10^{-24} \, \text{J/T}\). For small \(m\), say \(|m_l| \le 1\), the maximum energy shift magnitude is:
\[
|\langle H \rangle| \approx \mu_B B \approx 9.274 \times 10^{-24} \, \text{J} \approx 5.79 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{eV}.
\]

Comparing:
\[
5.79 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{eV} \ll 2.55 \, \text{eV}.
\]

Thus the paramagnetic coupling is much smaller than the transition energy.

