**Second reaction:**  
1-nitropropane + (KOH, (E)-but-2-enenitrile, H2O).  
This is a nitro-Michael addition. The nitro group stabilizes the carbanion α to it. 1-nitropropane has the nitro group on C1, so deprotonation occurs at C1 (the carbon bearing NO2). The resulting carbanion adds to the activated alkene (E)-but-2-enenitrile. The nitrile is at one end, and the alkene is 2-butene. The addition will place the nitropropane chain at the β-position relative to the nitrile. The product after aqueous workup gives a nitrile with a nitro group on the adjacent carbon. The original nitropropane contributes a three-carbon chain; the butenenitrile contributes a four-carbon chain with a nitrile at one end. The product is 3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile (the methyl comes from the internal alkene substitution). That matches choices A and B for B.

Combining: A = ethyl 3-(3-ethyl-3,5-dimethyl-4-oxocyclohexyl)propanoate, B = 3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile → choice B.

