We want eigenvector for eigenvalue \(+\hbar/2\). Solve:  
\[
\frac{\hbar}{2} \begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta & \sin\theta \\ \sin\theta & -\cos\theta\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}a\\b\end{pmatrix} = \frac{\hbar}{2} \begin{pmatrix}a\\b\end{pmatrix}.
\]  
Canceling \(\hbar/2\):  
\[
\begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta & \sin\theta \\ \sin\theta & -\cos\theta\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}a\\b\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}a\\b\end{pmatrix}.
\]  
This gives equations:  
\[
a\cos\theta + b\sin\theta = a, \quad a\sin\theta - b\cos\theta = b.
\]  
From first: \(b\sin\theta = a(1-\cos\theta)\). Using \(1-\cos\theta = 2\sin^2(\theta/2)\) and \(\sin\theta = 2\sin(\theta/2)\cos(\theta/2)\), we get  
\[
b\cdot 2\sin(\theta/2)\cos(\theta/2) = a\cdot 2\sin^2(\theta/2) \implies b\cos(\theta/2) = a\sin(\theta/2).
\]  
So \(a = \cos(\theta/2)\), \(b = \sin(\theta/2)\) works (up to normalization). Normalized:  
\[
\sqrt{\cos^2(\theta/2)+\sin^2(\theta/2)} = 1,
\]  
so the normalized eigenvector is \((\cos(\theta/2), \sin(\theta/2))\).  

That matches choice B.

