Product 1 is then treated with excess diethylaminosulfur trifluoride (DAST). DAST converts alcohols to alkyl fluorides with inversion, and also can convert carbonyls to geminal difluorides. Here, the ketone carbonyl will be converted to a CF₂ group, and the alcohol will be replaced by fluorine with inversion.  

Thus, the final product has a cyclohexane ring with two fluorines at the former carbonyl carbon (C2, gem-difluoro), and a fluoromethyl group attached at C1 (the former α-carbon) with a phenyl substituent. The stereochemistry: the original alcohol carbon had a specific configuration from the aldol; inversion by DAST gives the opposite configuration at that carbon. The other chiral center (the carbon bearing the phenyl and fluorine) will have the opposite configuration relative to the original alcohol.  

