[73/92] GPQA Diamond/Physics Consider an oscillating charge distribution, which is spheroid
We need to determine the angular dependence and wavelength scaling for radiation from an oscillating spheroidal charge distribution.  
For a radiating source that is not a simple dipole, the angular distribution can vary.  
If the spheroid oscillates along its symmetry axis (z-axis), the lowest-order radiation is likely electric dipole radiation, which has a \(\sin^2\theta\) angular dependence.  
The power per unit solid angle for dipole radiation is proportional to \(\sin^2\theta\).  
At \(\theta = 30^\circ\), \(\sin^2 30^\circ = (1/2)^2 = 1/4\).  
Thus the fraction of maximum power (which occurs at \(\theta=90^\circ\)) is \(1/4\).  

For the wavelength dependence: dipole radiation power scales as \(\lambda^{-4}\) (from Larmor formula and \(P \propto \omega^4 \propto \lambda^{-4}\)).  

Thus the fraction is \(1/4\) and the form is \(\lambda^{-4}\).  

